{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall count for yourselves.</b> Like 'and you shall take for yourselves' — that there should be a counting and a taking for each and every individual, that he count with his mouth and recite, according to the received tradition of our Rabbis of blessed memory. And what is written regarding the zav and zavah (Leviticus 15:13) 'and he shall count for him,' 'and she shall count for her,' is not of this matter, but rather to say that they should not forget it." "<b>And behold,</b> the essence of this commandment is that one begin to count from the sixteenth of Nisan, which is the beginning of the barley harvest, and bring the first of his harvest as a meal offering of fresh grain to HaShem, blessed be He, and offer a sacrifice upon it. And this count he completes at the beginning of the wheat harvest, and then he brings from it fine flour of wheat as a meal offering to HaShem, blessed be He, and offers a sacrifice upon it. Now the meal offerings are the essence of the commandment, not the sacrifices, for the sacrifices came only on account of the meal offerings. And the word 'new' is to say that they may not bring a meal offering in the House of HaShem until they bring this meal offering of wheat on the fiftieth day, which is the Festival of Shavuot. And He added, in mentioning the cessation of this festival, 'a perpetual statute throughout your generations in all your dwellings,' because the waving of the omer and the new meal offering are dependent on this festival; and it comes to teach you that even in this time, when there is no omer and no meal offering, the Torah commands the prohibition of new grain and the cessation of the Festival of Shavuot. And since He mentioned it regarding this festival, He did not need to mention it on the Day of Remembrance or on the Festival of Sukkot, for all the festivals have one and the same law regarding their obligation, whether in the Land or outside the Land. And what He mentioned regarding Yom HaKippurim is because our atonement on it depends on the sacrifices, and therefore He needed to mention 'a perpetual statute,' to teach you that the sacrifices are not indispensable. And likewise He did not mention here, at the Festival of Matzot, 'a perpetual statute throughout your dwellings throughout your generations'; but in the section of 'This month shall be for you' He did mention thus, because there He tied the matter to Pesach — 'on matzot and bitter herbs they shall eat it' — He needed to say that it shall be 'a perpetual statute throughout your dwellings.' Thus wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:50.132916274Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}